# UnityEngine vs AOSharp Ambiguity

`AOSharp.Common.GameData` and `UnityEngine` define overlapping type names. When both namespaces are in scope, the compiler reports CS0104 ambiguous reference errors.

## When editing C# files

After adding or changing `using` directives, check for types shared by both namespaces:

- `Mathf`, `Vector3`, `Vector2`, `Quaternion`, `Color`, `Rect`, `Matrix4x4`

## Fix pattern

Qualify with the namespace you intend:

```csharp
// Unity math / rendering
UnityEngine.Mathf.Max(a, b);
UnityEngine.Vector3.zero;

// AO protocol / game data structs (when intentionally using AO types)
AOSharp.Common.GameData.Vector3 aoPos = ...;
```

Prefer `UnityEngine.*` in Unity scripts (MonoBehaviours, UI, rendering, physics). Use AOSharp types only when converting to or from network/game-data messages.

## Existing project conventions

- Some files use aliases: `using Vector3 = UnityEngine.Vector3;`
- Some files already qualify: `UnityEngine.Vector3 localPlayerPos`
- Match the style of the file you are editing when one is already established.

## Before finishing

If a file has `using AOSharp.Common.GameData;` and `using UnityEngine;`, scan for unqualified uses of the shared types above and fix any ambiguity.
